<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Osun-Osogbo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Osun-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a> Sacred Grove is a forest along the Osun River just outside Osogbo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, sacred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oshun">Oshun</a>. Several centuries old, it is among the last of the sacred forests that once bordered most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> cities. In the 1950s its shrines were neglected and it was fished, hunted and felled, until the Austrian artist Susanne Wenger, with the support of the Ataoja, the king of Osogbo, and local people, founded the New Sacred Art movement to restore and protect it. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2005, and the Osun-Osogbo Festival is held there every August.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osun-osogbo.jpg" alt="Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove" width="330" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ABORISADEADETONA · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osun_groove_Osogbo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Every August the <b>Osun-Osogbo</b> festival draws thousands of worshippers and visitors to the river goddess’s sacred grove.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
